1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,860
In 1997, the Pacific Ocean made a

2
00:00:03,880 --> 00:00:05,950
sound no one could explain.

3
00:00:06,500 --> 00:00:10,190
NOAA scientists were listening for underwater volcanoes.

4
00:00:11,060 --> 00:00:15,010
Microphones spread more than 2,000 miles apart,

5
00:00:15,180 --> 00:00:17,490
picked up the same powerful rumble,

6
00:00:18,120 --> 00:00:19,970
became known as the Bloop.

7
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:23,530
Here it is, sped up 16 times.

8
00:00:27,360 --> 00:00:30,110
The theories piled up.

9
00:00:30,520 --> 00:00:32,350
Secret military test.

10
00:00:33,360 --> 00:00:34,210
Ship's engine.

11
00:00:35,200 --> 00:00:35,970
Giant squid.

12
00:00:36,860 --> 00:00:38,930
Something unknown to science.

13
00:00:39,500 --> 00:00:41,450
For years, nobody knew.

14
00:00:42,120 --> 00:00:45,020
Then researchers moved their microphones closer to

15
00:00:45,040 --> 00:00:48,900
Antarctica, and in 2005 they found the

16
00:00:48,920 --> 00:00:49,470
source.

17
00:00:50,000 --> 00:00:52,050
The Bloop was an icequake,

18
00:00:52,380 --> 00:00:55,380
an iceberg cracking and breaking away from

19
00:00:55,400 --> 00:00:56,810
an Antarctic glacier.

20
00:00:57,460 --> 00:00:59,270
The monster was ice.
